# Pilot: an agent-played game of Dynasty. Five players: the peasant game's table.
# Every say and aside is the reply of the agent playing that seat (see log/yearN/).
# The Storyteller writes the act lines and refs the moves; the engine keeps the books.
# The narrator's lines are not part of the game: they live in narration.md, never here.
# Each chapter opens with a "# ===== CHAPTER N: <title>" marker. Seed and deck order: seed.md.
# The trial year that came before this game is in trial-year1/.
setup([1,5,6,7,8], "peasant-collapse", "The peasant game",
      "Five players · eight years · ends in collapse",
      "The Emperor, three peasants and the Warlord, each played by its own agent. The Storyteller referees with the engine.",
      short="5 players · agents")

# ============================== SETUP ==============================
R(0, "Setup", None, "Five players: the Emperor, the three peasants and the Warlord (rulebook Section 4 and Appendix A). Nobody sits in Provinces 2, 3 or 4, so nothing is owed to them, and their table jobs go to whoever the Emperor names (Note 3).")
act("Five booklets: Reina Osorio draws the Emperor, Silke Weber the Silk Peasant, Paddy Doyle the Rice Peasant, Graham Garner the Wheat Peasant and Robert Land the Warlord.", map_hl=[1,5,6,7,8])
deal_grain("The Harvest Pile deals 60 to each player. It is the first year's harvest.")
take_stock("Silke, Paddy and Graham take their realia out of the box: one piece of their own and two of the piece they'll never need. It waits beside the board until it's sold.")
act("Graham cuts out the Province 7 Reservoir, two squares by two, and sets it aside until it's built.", map_hl=[7])
# From here the players' own choices: Rob's army and tax stance, Reina's appointments.
# ===== CHAPTER 1: The Only Bowl in China
place_army({"W1":"O18", "W2":"T20"}, "Robert Land stands his two soldiers on his desert: W1 on O18, the notch where Graham's Province 7 and Silke's Province 5 meet, and W2 on T20, on the sand just below Paddy's Province 6.",
 "Rob: O18 sits next to both Graham and Silke and T20 is right up against Paddy, so from Year 1 every peasant who's got a piece I need for my objective is one step from a raid. They can buy my soldiers off me for 20 if they like, and I'll take the fiver and smile, but I'm betting they'd rather spend their grain at the auctions.")
say("Rob", "Two lads, two lovely little camping spots. One's got a view of the wheat and the silk, the other one's just looking at the rice, and I'll be honest, Paddy, he's looking at it hungrily. And tax? Reina, love, the desert doesn't pay the Emperor. The Emperor pays the desert, she just doesn't know it yet.")
act("Robert Land says he won't pay the Emperor's tax on his harvest. The Warlord may refuse it.", map_hl=[8])
act("Reina hands out the jobs of the empty seats: Silke Weber banks the Harvest Pile, Graham Garner reads the I Ching card each year for 5 a year from the crown, and Paddy Doyle collects the tax.", map_hl=[1,5,6,7])
say("Reina", "Silke has the bank, Graham reads the card and gets his five a year, and Paddy collects, because nobody argues with a rice farmer holding a ledger. Forty percent, gentlemen, twenty-four apiece, and I'm building a palace with it. Rob, darling, I've written down every year you don't pay, and walls have long memories.",
 "Reina: Forty percent is enough for the Summer Palace and leaves a cushion, and it keeps the peasants a long way from revolt talk. The palace goes on F17, tucked into the corner as far from that soldier on O18 as I can get it, because it unlocks the Wall and my canal and I'm not paying Rob twenty to stand in my garden.")

# ============================== YEAR 1 ==============================
R(1, "The first year", None, "No card is drawn. The 60 each player was dealt is the first harvest, and the tax is levied on it.")
CUR_INC.update({s:60 for s in ACTIVE})
taxes(.40, collector="Paddy collects the tax for the crown")
act("Robert Land doesn't pay.", map_hl=[8])
build("Summer Palace", 20, {1:20}, key="Summer Palace", at="F17", text="Public works: Reina builds the Summer Palace on F17, the corner of Province 1 farthest from Rob's soldiers. It must stand before any Wall link or the Province 1 canal.", hover="Reina pays the 20.")
say("Silke", "One Dragon Jar, gentlemen, and only the one, so somebody goes home sad. Paddy, Graham, you both need it and you both know it. Six to open, and I'll be counting every grain into that Harvest Pile myself, honestly, since I'm the bank.",
 "Silke: There's one Jar and two boys who need it, so the scarce piece goes up first while they're both still flush, and a quarter of whatever they bid each other up to comes to me. The Customs Houses can wait. There are two of them and one each, so there's no fight over those. I'll need every grain I can get when Graham's Vase and Paddy's Bowl come round.")
act("Silke puts up her Dragon Jar, the only one in the game, with a minimum of 6. Paddy and Graham both need it.", map_hl=[5])
say("Rob", "A Dragon Jar? In my desert? It'd just fill up with sand. No, you boys go on, fight over it, bid it up nice and high. Wherever it ends up, it'll be standing on a field, and I know where all the fields are.",
 "Rob: I don't need to own the realia, I need to take it off people, and the moment that jar is bought it's sitting on someone's province within a few steps of my lads. Every grain Paddy and Graham burn bidding against each other is grain they can't spend buying my soldiers off me.")
lot(5, "DJ", [
 B(6, 6, "Graham, there's only the one jar, and if we go at each other over it the Harvest Pile eats us both. Let me have it near the six and you get first go at my Bamboo Brush at its minimum, no bid from me, my word on it in front of everyone. If not, well, I'm a rice farmer, I'm used to a long season.",
   "Paddy: The Dragon Jar is the only one there'll ever be, so I can't let it go. I'll go to twenty-two to keep it from Graham, but past that I'd be too poor to buy the Vase or the Customs House, and I've still to hold my twenty grain at the end."),
 B(7, 16, "Paddy. There's one jar and two of us, and every grain we throw at each other goes into Silke's pile, not our pockets. Stand aside on this one and my Vase is yours at the minimum when it comes up. Nobody bids against you, me included. That's the offer, and I'll say it once.",
   "Graham: I've got thirty-six grain and I have to finish holding twenty, so I can't win a war for this jar, but if Paddy gets the only one I'm out of the game. So I'll go up to sixteen against him and no further, and I'm hoping he takes the Vase instead."),
 B(6, 17), B(7, None, "Fine. Remember I offered.")],
 hover="Paddy puts the Jar on Q21, at the north edge of Province 6, out of both soldiers' reach for two rounds.", at="Q21")
say("Paddy", "A promise is a promise, Graham, even if you made me pay seventeen for that jar. One Bamboo Brush, two to open, and I'll not bid a grain against you. Silke, there's another one coming next round, so maybe let the man have this one quiet.",
 "Paddy: I'm down to nineteen and still owe myself a Vase, a Customs House and a Land Deed Scroll, so keeping my word to Graham costs me almost nothing and might get me his Vase cheap. I'm saving the Wooden Bowl for later, when Silke and Graham will both want it and the bidding pays better.")
act("Paddy puts up one of his two Bamboo Brushes at the minimum of 2, as he offered Graham before the Jar was sold.", map_hl=[6])
say("Silke", "Take it, Graham, I won't touch it. But everyone heard this: when the second Brush comes up, it's mine at the minimum. Graham won't need it by then, and Paddy, you'd be selling it to the woman who pays out your harvest.",
 "Silke: After this sale I'm the only one left who needs a Brush, so the second one comes to me at two whatever happens. There's no point making the bank richer by bidding against Graham now. I'm keeping my 40 for the Vase and the Bowl, because that's where the real fights will be.")
say("Rob", "Look at this, honour among farmers. Lovely. Paddy, that's a handsome jar you've got sat out on Q21, by the way. Very shiny. You can see it from the desert.",
 "Rob: I'm not getting between Paddy and Graham's little pact, because the brush ends up on Graham's fields either way and that's where I want it. My W2 at T20 is three steps from Paddy's jar, and Paddy's down to 19 grain, which isn't enough to buy my soldier off me and still pay his tax.")
lot(6, "BB", [
 B(7, 2, "Two. Thank you, Paddy. You kept your word, I'll give you that. Silke, there's a second one next round. I'd be grateful if you waited for it.",
   "Graham: The only jar is standing on Paddy's field now, and he's not giving it up, so this brush only matters if the jar ever moves. I'll pay a little for it and not a grain more.")],
 hover="Nobody else bids. Graham puts the Brush on R14, in the south-west of Province 7, out of both soldiers' reach for two rounds.", at="R14")
say("Graham", "My offer on the Vase had a condition. Paddy, you were to stand aside on the jar, and you didn't, so that offer is gone. A Land Deed Scroll, two to open. There's one each for Silke and Paddy, so there's no need to fight over it.",
 "Graham: The Vase is the only one, and Paddy needs it the same way I need the jar on his field, so I'm holding it back until he wants to talk about a trade. A cheap scroll keeps me within the rules for now and doesn't cost me anything.")
act("Graham puts up one of his two Land Deed Scrolls, minimum 2, and keeps the Vase back for a later round.", map_hl=[7])
say("Rob", "Oh, Graham's cross with Paddy, Silke's cross with Graham, and Paddy's down to his last nineteen and a half. Isn't this a happy table? Go on, sell your scrolls. I'm not bidding, I'm just enjoying the show.",
 "Rob: Every piece that lands on the board is one more thing for my lads to pick up. Graham's brush on R14 is four steps from W1, and the three peasants are already squabbling over grudges instead of thinking about who's camped on their border.")
lot(7, "LD", [
 B(5, 2, "There are two Scrolls and two of us, Paddy, so let's not feed the pile. I'll take this one at two, you take the next one at two, and I won't bid on yours. You've only got nineteen and a half left anyway, so save it for the Vase.",
   "Silke: There are two Scrolls for two buyers, so I've capped myself at five. If Paddy wants to throw grain at this one, he can have it and I'll get the second cheap."),
 B(6, 4, "Fair enough, Graham, I did bid on the jar, I can't argue that. Silke, here's a deal for you: let me have this Scroll at two, and the second Brush is yours at the minimum next round, nobody else gets it, my word, same as I gave Graham. Then the second Scroll's yours and nobody's out of pocket.",
   "Paddy: I've nineteen and a half and I have to finish on twenty, so I can't afford a fight with anyone. Swapping Silke the Brush for a quiet Scroll keeps the Harvest Pile from eating my supper."),
 B(5, 5),
 B(6, None, "Grand, Silke, it's yours, and I'll hold you to the other half: the next Scroll's mine at two and you keep your hands in your pockets. Everyone heard it.",
   "Paddy: Fighting Silke for this one only feeds the pile, and there's a second Scroll coming that she's promised to leave alone. I'm keeping my nineteen and a half for the Vase and the Customs House.")],
 hover="Silke: I'm putting mine on O22, deep in the coast, where Rob's soldiers can't reach it.", at="O22")
act("Paddy offered Silke the second Brush in return for this Scroll; it went to Silke instead. What stands, as talk, is Silke's word that she won't bid on Graham's second Scroll.", map_hl=[5,6])
march({"W1":"O19", "W2":"S21"}, "Robert Land marches: W1 steps from the notch onto O19 and raids Silke's Province 5; W2 steps up from the desert onto S21 and raids Paddy's Province 6. Five from each.",
 "Rob: Each of the three pieces I want now has its own soldier walking at it: W1 is three steps from Silke's scroll, W2 is two from Paddy's jar, and the new lad on T14 is two from Graham's brush. Paddy's got nineteen and a half grain, so he can't buy my soldier off me without starving, and the other two are going to have to decide quickly how much their pottery's worth to them.")
hire_soldier("T14", "Robert Land hires a third soldier, W3, for 20, paid to the Harvest Pile, and stands it on T14, on the desert two squares below Graham's Bamboo Brush. It moves from next year.")
say("Rob", "Morning, Silke. Morning, Paddy. That'll be five each, thank you, call it a visitor's fee. And Graham, don't feel left out, I've just hired a nice young man to stand at the bottom of your garden.")
salaries({7:5}, text="Reina pays Graham 5 for reading the cards, as she said she would.", hover="Nobody else at this table is on a salary.")

# ============================== YEAR 2 ==============================
R(2, "The canal gives way", "canal")
act("Graham turns the first I Ching card: The Canal Gives Way. An average harvest for every province. No canal has been dug, so there's none to lose. Provinces 2, 3, 7 and 8 play a personal disaster; 2 and 3 are empty, so that's Graham and Rob.", map_hl=[7,8])
harvest("Silke pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile.")
disaster(7, "a hailstorm flattens a corner of the wheat.")
disaster(8, "wounded in the legs. No raids on Provinces 5, 6 or 7 this year.")
say("Reina", "Forty percent again, friends. I said steady and I meant it, so nobody at this table gets surprised by me. And the crown is digging a canal this year. Rob, I hear you've hurt your legs. Rest them. It's a long walk to Province 1.",
 "Reina: A Province 1 canal section adds ten to my own harvest every year from next year, so it pays for itself in four years and it keeps paying. The Winter Palace and the Reservoir can wait a year or two, but the five hundred I still owe won't wait, so I start the projects that earn back first.")
taxes(.40, collector="Paddy collects the tax for the crown")
act("Robert Land doesn't pay.", map_hl=[8])
build("Grand Canal", 40, {1:40}, canal={1:1}, text="Public works: Reina digs the first section of the Grand Canal in Province 1. It adds 10 to her harvest from next year.", hover="Reina pays the 40. 480 of the works are still owed.")
say("Silke", "One Customs House, two to open. There's one for each of you, boys, so there's nothing to fight over. I'm keeping it cheap because I've got a long memory too, Reina, and I'd like these two to remember who was kind to them when the Vase and the Bowl come round.",
 "Silke: I can't get anything worth having out of a Customs House that both buyers can have, so I'm trading it for goodwill. What I really want is the Bowl and the Vase, and I've kept fifty-one grain to get them.")
act("Silke puts up one of her two Customs Houses, minimum 2. Paddy and Graham need one each.", map_hl=[5])
say("Rob", "Bad legs, Reina, not a bad memory. I'll rest them this year, and so will you all, I expect. Enjoy it. Customs House? Nothing to declare, Silke, I'm sat in the sand.",
 "Rob: The wound means I can't raid this year, so I'm standing my lads right where they are, two or three steps from the jar, the scroll and the brush, while the peasants spend. More realia on the board just gives me more to choose from next year.")
lot(5, "CH", [
 B(7, 2, "Thank you, Silke. Paddy, there are two of these. I'll take this one cheap, and I won't bid on the next.",
   "Graham: There's no reason to fight over something that comes in pairs. I want this one cheap and safe at P14, away from that new soldier at the bottom of my garden, and I'm saving my grain for when Paddy finally wants to talk about the Vase and the jar."),
 B(6, 3, "Thank you, Silke, that's kindly done. Graham, I know I owe you one after the jar, so here's the fairest deal I can make: there are two Houses, so let me have this one at two and I won't bid a grain on the second. Or if you'd rather have this one, say so now and I'll wait for the next. Either way neither of us feeds the pile.",
   "Paddy: I'll go to six and no further. I've thirty-five and a half, and I still need a Scroll, the Vase and twenty in my pocket at the end."),
 B(7, 6), B(6, 6),
 B(7, None, "Take it at six, Paddy. Everyone heard you: you won't bid a grain on the second one. I'll be counting.",
   "Graham: Paddy offered me this one and then bid against me anyway, and that's the second time. I'll let him have it, because another bidding war only feeds the pile, but I'm keeping track of what he owes me, and the Vase is going to cost him.")],
 hover=TH(("", "Both limits stood at six, and a tie goes to the earlier seat: Paddy, in Province 6, before Graham in 7."),
           ("Paddy", "Rob's soldier is sitting right on S21, so whatever I win goes down on Q24 where he can't reach it.")), at="Q24")
say("Paddy", "The Wooden Bowl, friends, the only one in China, four to open. Silke, Graham, you both need it, so I'll leave it to the two of you, and you'd do well to settle it between you before you start feeding the pile. Silke, the Brush comes next year, and it's yours at the minimum, same as I said.",
 "Paddy: I need every half grain I can get before Graham's Vase comes round, and the Bowl is the only thing I own that two people want. A quarter of a good price beats a quarter of two, and promising Silke the Brush keeps her friendly while I do it.")
act("Paddy puts up the Wooden Bowl, the only one in the game, minimum 4. Silke and Graham both need it.", map_hl=[6])
say("Rob", "The only bowl in all of China, and two hungry farmers. Silke, Graham, settle it nicely or don't, I'm easy either way. Whoever gets it, put it somewhere pretty. Somewhere close to the edge, ideally.",
 "Rob: Paddy's just put a second piece on the board at Q24, so I've got two of his to pick from now, and whichever of Silke or Graham wins this bowl gives me another one. I'm keeping my 60 for soldiers and letting them pay the pile.")
lot(6, "WB", [
 B(5, 4, "Graham, I'm the only one in China with a Customs House left, and I set its minimum. Stand aside on the Bowl and it's yours next year at two, my word, in front of everyone. If you bid against me here, don't be surprised when that minimum turns out to be twenty.",
   "Silke: Paddy's promised not to bid on the last Customs House, so I'm the only one who can sell it to Graham, and the minimum is my price to name. I'll go to twenty for the Bowl. After that I need to keep enough for the Vase and still finish holding twenty grain."),
 B(7, 5, "Silke. You need the Vase, and I'm the only one who has it. Stand aside on this Bowl and the Vase is yours next round at the minimum, and I'll keep Paddy off it. This offer has a condition too, and I'll hold you to it exactly.",
   "Graham: I'm trading the Vase for the Bowl, which is the only thing at this table I can still bargain with. If Silke doesn't take the deal, I've got the grain to outlast her, and I'll still keep the twenty I need at the end."),
 B(5, 20), B(7, 21),
 B(5, 25, "Your Vase is no good to me without the Bowl, Graham, and you know it, so that offer was empty. Twenty-five. Every grain past this goes into the pile I pay out, so keep going if you like feeding Rob's harvest.",
   "Silke: There's only one Bowl. Whoever doesn't get it is stuck, so I'm not going to give it away for a Vase I can't use without it. I'm stopping at twenty-five."),
 B(7, None, "Twenty-five. Take it, Silke. And you're right, the Vase is no good to you without the Bowl. Now that you have the Bowl, it's no good to you without the Vase. Next round, we'll see what it's worth.",
   "Graham: If I'd paid twenty-six, Silke would have charged me twenty for the Customs House and the jar would still be sitting on Paddy's field, so I'd have finished broke. She has the Bowl now and I have the only Vase, and from now on she's the one who has to ask me for something.")],
 hover="Silke puts the Bowl on O23, beside her Scroll, out of every soldier's reach for two rounds.", at="O23")
say("Graham", "My last Land Deed Scroll, two to open. Paddy, that's yours, and Silke has already given her word she won't bid. The Vase comes next year. Silke, your Customs House goes up before my Vase does. Set its minimum however you like. I'll be setting mine afterwards.",
 "Graham: The Vase comes up last next year, after her Customs House, so whatever price Silke puts on that House, I can put on the only Vase in China. She can be reasonable with me, or we can both lose grain to the pile.")
act("Graham puts up his last Land Deed Scroll, minimum 2. Only the Vase is left in his stock, so it must go up next year.", map_hl=[7])
say("Silke", "I gave my word, so it's Paddy's. Graham, you need my Customs House and I need your Vase, and we're the only seller of each. Both at two next year, in front of everyone. Or we can both set them at thirty and let Rob laugh at us.",
 "Silke: Graham and I each hold the one piece the other can't do without. Making a fair swap is cheaper for both of us than the bidding war we just had over the Bowl. I've only got twenty-seven and a half, and I have to end on twenty, so I can't afford another fight.")
say("Rob", "Twenty-five for a bowl. Silke, I'm touched, honestly. Half of that's going into the Harvest Pile, and the harvest pays me too. Go on, Paddy, take your scroll. I'm not in the paperwork business.",
 "Rob: Silke's got three pieces lined up along row O now, at O22 and O23, with W1 sat three steps away, and she's only got 27 and a half grain left, so she can afford to buy exactly one of my soldiers. Graham and Silke at each other's throats over the Vase is the best thing that could have happened to me.")
act("Of Silke's 25 for the Bowl, 19 went to the Harvest Pile, three quarters of it less the rounding, and Paddy kept 6.", map_hl=[5,6])
lot(7, "LD", [
 B(6, 2, "Thank you, Graham, that's decent of you, and I'll not forget it. Two it is, and if anyone fancies bidding it up just to watch me sweat, you'll only be feeding the pile.",
   "Paddy: Nobody else needs a Scroll, so it should come to me cheap. I'll go to eight in case Rob gets cheeky. Then it's only the Vase, and Silke and I will both be after it.")],
 hover="Nobody else bids. Paddy puts the Scroll on Q25, the corner of Province 6 farthest from the soldiers.", at="Q25")
act("Robert Land's soldiers stand still: W1 on O19, W2 on S21, W3 on T14. His wound forbids raids on Provinces 5, 6 and 7 this year.",
 "Rob: Moving can't gain me anything this year, so everyone stays put: W2 is two steps from Paddy's jar, W3 two from Graham's brush, and W1 three from Silke's scroll. Next year, once the legs heal, all three can arrive at about the same time, and the peasants can't afford to buy off more than one or two of them.", map_hl=[8])
say("Rob", "The lads are resting their legs, just like Her Majesty told them to. Sitting on your doorsteps, Silke, Paddy, Graham, enjoying the view. Don't mind them, they'll be gone soon enough, and something of yours will go with them.")
salaries({7:5}, text="Reina pays Graham his 5 for reading the cards.")

# ===== CHAPTER 2: A Lovely Thing to Lose
# ============================== YEAR 3 ==============================
R(3, "The villages grow", "half2")
act("Graham turns the I Ching card: Population Grows by Half. A good harvest for every province, and from this year every province eats half as much again: 60 for a peasant, 90 for the Emperor, 30 for the Warlord.", map_hl=[1,5,6,7,8])
harvest("Silke pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile.")
say("Reina", "Forty percent again, my dears, same as the last two years, so nobody here gets surprised by me, even with more mouths to feed. The Winter Palace goes up on G18 this year, and after that the canals come down the coast to Silke and Paddy. Rob, darling, that's year three you haven't paid. I've written it down.",
 "Reina: The Winter Palace is the cheapest thing I owe, and building it is what lets me dig the coastal canals. Every canal makes Silke and Paddy richer and easier to tax, and keeps them glad I'm on the throne. Rob's men are still five rounds from my wall, so I'll hold on to my grain and only buy that soldier when one of them actually starts walking north.")
taxes(.40, collector="Paddy collects the tax for the crown")
act("Robert Land doesn't pay.", map_hl=[8])
build("Winter Palace", 20, {1:20}, key="Winter Palace", at="G18", text="Public works: Reina builds the Winter Palace on G18, beside the Summer Palace. With it standing, the Grand Canal may now be dug in Provinces 5 and 6.", hover="Reina pays the 20. 440 of the works are still owed.")
say("Silke", "Two, Graham, like I promised. I said both at two, and I've done my half in front of everyone, so Reina can write it down. Your Vase comes up after Paddy's Brush, and the whole table will be watching to see whether a Garner keeps a deal as well as a Weber does. Paddy, you're welcome to bid on my Customs House, but you've already got one and it'd only be feeding Rob's pile.",
 "Silke: I've no grain left for a war, so I'm going to win this one by shaming him. If Graham gets the last Customs House for two and then charges me thirty for the Vase, every peasant at this table will know he can't be trusted, and he'll need friends when Rob's legs heal.")
act("Silke puts up her last Customs House, minimum 2. Graham is the only one who still needs one; Paddy already has his. Last year Silke offered Graham this House and his Vase both at two.", map_hl=[5,7])
say("Paddy", "I said I'd keep off Silke's Customs House and I will. It's yours, Graham, at two, and good luck to you. And Graham, I know I owe you one for the Jar. I haven't forgotten it.",
 "Paddy: I've already got a Customs House, so bidding on this one would only break my word and fatten Rob's pile. If Graham gets it cheap and I keep my hands off it in front of everyone, maybe he'll be a bit kinder to me when his Vase comes up.")
say("Rob", "Look at that. Three farmers, one customs house, and nobody's thrown the bowl yet. I'm almost proud of you. Go on, Graham, have it for two. That leaves you a bit more to spare for my visitor's fee.",
 "Rob: That's every lot I've passed on, and I've still got all seventy, which is more than any of the farmers. Paddy's jar is two steps from W1 and two from W2, so this is the year the desert comes calling.")
lot(5, "CH", [
 B(7, 2, "Two, then. Thank you, Silke. I'll note that you kept it. Paddy, I've noted yours too. The Vase comes up after the Brush, and it'll go up at the price that was agreed.",
   "Graham: I'm the only one who needs this Customs House, so fifteen only matters if Rob decides to make me pay for it, and Q15 is a square his soldiers can't reach for two years. I've kept a list of who kept their word, and so far it's a short one.")],
 hover="Nobody else bids. Graham puts the Customs House on Q15.", at="Q15")
say("Paddy", "Two, Silke, like I promised last year. A Doyle keeps his word as well as a Weber or a Garner does. Graham, when your Vase comes up I'll bid on it honestly and in the open, and whatever happens there'll be no hard feelings from me.",
 "Paddy: Honestly I'd rather squeeze Silke on this one, since she's my rival for the Vase, but I promised her two and the whole table heard me say it. I've got 57 and a half grain and need to finish on 20, so I can afford a good bid on that Vase now, though I fear Silke's already bought it with her Customs House.")
act("Paddy puts up his last Bamboo Brush, minimum 2, as he promised Silke last year. Silke is the only one who still needs one; Graham has his.", map_hl=[6,5])
say("Graham", "I've got a brush already, so I'll stay out of it. Silke kept her word on the Customs House and I'm not going to push up the price of hers. The Vase is next.",
 "Graham: I could bid this up to hurt Silke, but she owns the Bowl I need, and every grain I spent here would go to the pile. I'm keeping my grain for the Vase, because that's the one thing everyone at this table needs from me.")
say("Rob", "Relax, Silke, I don't write letters. Have your brush for two. Mind you, it's a lovely thing to own, and a lovely thing to lose.",
 "Rob: Every piece I let them buy cheap leaves more grain in their pockets for my visitor's fee. And Graham's new customs house on Q15 is only three steps from W3, so he's just built me a second front door.")
lot(6, "BB", [
 B(5, 2, "Two, and thank you, Paddy. You said it last year and you kept it, and I'll say so to anyone who asks. Rob, if you want to bid on a brush you've no use for, go ahead, but every grain you put in goes to the Harvest Pile and not to you.",
   "Silke: Nobody else needs the Brush, so the ceiling only matters if Rob decides to make trouble, and ten is all I'll give him. It goes on O25 next to the Scroll and the Bowl, deep on the coast where his soldiers can't get at it.")],
 hover="Nobody else bids. Silke puts the Brush on O25.", at="O25")
say("Graham", "Two, the price Silke and I agreed. A Garner keeps his word, so you can write that down as well, Reina. I promised the minimum, nothing more, so the two of you can settle the rest between you.",
 "Graham: I set the minimum at two because I said I would, and now Silke and Paddy each need this Vase to finish a set. Whichever one loses will have learned what it's like to be on my list, and that one might still sell me what I'm missing.")
act("Graham puts up the Vase, the last piece in his stock and the only Vase in the game, minimum 2. Silke and Paddy each need only the Vase: whoever buys it holds a complete set.", map_hl=[7,5,6])
say("Rob", "Oh, don't mind me, I've got the best seat in the house. Two farmers, one pot, and the whole game riding on it. Go on, Paddy, Silke, bid like you mean it. Every grain you throw in is one less you can use to buy off my lads.",
 "Rob: I'd love to stick my oar in and push the price up, but if I get stuck holding that Vase I've spent my own grain on a pot I don't need. Whoever ends up with it will be the one with a full set and an empty purse, and that's who I'll go and see first.")
lot(7, "VA", [
 B(5, 2, "Paddy, last year I stood aside on the Scroll so you could have it at two, and the whole table watched me do it. I'm asking you to do the same for me now. If we fight over it, every grain we bid goes into the pile and Rob gets to watch two farmers ruin each other over one pot.",
   "Silke: Twenty-eight is my limit. Above that I'm not safely over my twenty, and a full set's no use to me if I'm too poor to win with it. Paddy has eight grain more than I do, so I'm counting on him remembering that Scroll."),
 B(6, 3, "Silke, I won't pretend I didn't notice you standing aside on that Scroll. But you'd already got a Scroll of your own on O22, so it cost you nothing, and this pot is the whole game for both of us. I can't give you the whole game for a favour like that. I'll bid fair and in the open, and whoever ends up with the Vase, the other one shakes hands on it.",
   "Paddy: I've got 58, so I can go to 36 and still keep 22, just over the 20 I need to finish. Silke's only got 50, so she can't go past 30 without spending herself out of the win. It's a cruel thing to say no to her, but I've got a long season behind me and I'm not going to hand it away now."),
 B(5, 28),
 B(6, 29),
 B(5, None, "Take it, Paddy. And remember I'm the one who held the bank while you did it.")],
 hover="Paddy puts the Vase on S24, in the corner of Province 6 farthest from the soldiers.", at="S24")
act("Paddy now holds the Dragon Jar, a Customs House, a Land Deed Scroll and the Vase, with 29 grain: a complete set and the 20 to go with it. A peasant with a complete set may change seats with another peasant.", map_hl=[6], big=True)
say("Paddy", "Silke, I'm sorry, truly I am, but I'll be taking the Silk province now. You'll have sixty grain and my old rice fields, and they're good fields, I promise you. They've been kind to me. Rob, your lads can have a look at Province 6 if they like, but I won't be there.",
 "Paddy: Province 5's a lower seat than the one I drew, so moving up the coast is a win, and doing it now means W1 and W2 never get the chance to grab my Jar on Q21. It's the Silk Peasant's own piece anyway, so I'd have to sell it next year whatever happened. I've put everything on O22 to O24, out of every soldier's reach for two rounds, and I've 79 grain, so I'm well clear of my twenty.")
swap(6, 5, "Paddy Doyle shows his set and his 20 and replaces Silke Weber as the Silk Peasant in Province 5. He takes her 50 grain on top of his own 29, and keeps the Customs House, the Scroll and the Vase, on O22, O23 and O24. The Dragon Jar is Province 5's own piece, so it goes into his stock, to be auctioned next year. Silke returns her Scroll, Bowl and Brush, draws 60 from the Harvest Pile and becomes the Rice Peasant in Province 6.",
 hover="Silke's Bowl and Brush go into Province 6's stock, now hers to sell; her Scroll goes into Graham's stock, since Province 3, where Scrolls come from, is empty. Paddy drew seat 6 and now holds seat 5.",
 at={"CH":"O22","LD":"O23","VA":"O24"})
march({"W1":"O20", "W2":"R22", "W3":"S14"}, "Robert Land marches: W1 steps onto O20 and raids Paddy's Province 5; W2 steps onto R22 and raids Silke's Province 6; W3 steps onto S14 and raids Graham's Province 7, the first raid there. Five from each.",
 "Rob: All three lads step in and take their five on the way. W1 is now two steps off Paddy's customs house and W3 is one off Graham's brush. Paddy's a big man with seventy-nine grain, so he's the one I'm going to rob.")
say("Rob", "Congratulations, Paddy, a full set and a new house on the coast! Shame you've put every piece you own in one tidy row on O22, O23 and O24. I'll be round with a housewarming present. Silke, love, welcome to the rice fields. Visitor's fee's five, same as it ever was. Graham, keep that brush polished, my lad's nearly at your gate.")
salaries({7:5}, text="Reina pays Graham his 5 for reading the cards.")

# ============================== YEAR 4 ==============================
R(4, "A bad harvest", "dribble2")
act("Graham turns the I Ching card: Bad Harvest. Every province collects a bad harvest, and with half as many mouths again to feed, every province eats more than it grows this year.", map_hl=[1,5,6,7,8])
harvest("Silke pays out the harvest; this year every province pays into the Harvest Pile instead.")
say("Reina", "Forty percent, as always. It's worth nothing in a year like this, but I said the rate wouldn't move, and it won't. I'm digging my own second canal section with my own grain. Congratulations on the coast, Paddy. Silke, the rice fields will treat you well. Rob, darling, that's year four on the tally.",
 "Reina: In a famine the cheapest work is the one that pays me back, so I'm putting my second canal in now, while the peasants are too hungry to blame me for anything. The coastal canals can wait until I know whether Paddy and Silke stay friends with me or with each other.")
taxes(.40, collector="Paddy collects the tax for the crown")
build("Grand Canal", 40, {1:40}, canal={1:1}, text="Public works: Reina digs the second section of the Grand Canal in Province 1. It adds another 10 to her harvest from next year.", hover="Reina pays the 40. 400 of the works are still owed.")
say("Paddy", "Two, and that's all I'm asking. Silke, Graham, you both need it, so settle it between you however you think is fair. I'd rather it went cheap than see half the jar go into the Harvest Pile over a quarrel. Silke, I haven't forgotten you held the bank for me. And Rob, you'll get no housewarming fee out of me if I can help it.",
 "Paddy: I've got a lower seat than the one I drew, so I've no reason to move again. Swapping with Silke or Graham would only send me back down the coast. So I'll sell the jar cheap, keep the peace, and sit tight on my 64 while Rob's lads come knocking.")
act("Paddy puts up the Dragon Jar, the only one, minimum 2. It is Province 5's own piece, so the Silk Peasant must sell it. Graham needs it, and so does Silke now that she is the Rice Peasant. Silke's stock holds the Wooden Bowl, which Graham also needs.", "At auction three quarters of the price goes to the Harvest Pile; the seller keeps a quarter.", map_hl=[5,6,7])
say("Rob", "Here we go again, another year and another pot to fight over. Graham's going to bid all the way, Silke's going to set the bowl at whatever he's got left, and Paddy sits on the coast saying 'settle it fairly.' Lovely stuff. Paddy, love, about that housewarming fee: my lad on O20 is two steps from your front door, and he's never needed an invitation.",
 "Rob: The jar's no use to me, but every grain Graham and Silke throw at it goes in the pile and not into buying off my boys. Meanwhile W3 is one step from Graham's brush, and I'm the only one at this table who isn't paying for anything this year.")
lot(5, "DJ", [
 B(6, 2, "Graham, before you bid, I've got the only Wooden Bowl in China in my stock now, and you can't finish without it. Let me have this Jar at two and the Bowl goes up at two next year, my word in front of Reina's book. Bid me up on it, and the Bowl's minimum will be whatever you've got left.",
   "Silke: I've got forty-five grain and Graham's got eighty-three, so I can't win a bidding war against him. What I've got is the Bowl, and I'm making sure he knows what it'll cost him to beat me here. Twenty-two is as far as I'll go; past that I'd rather take my quarter of his money for the Bowl later."),
 B(7, 3, "Silke, there's one Vase in China and it's on Paddy's square at O24. Without it a jar is no use to you, it's just a pot. I'm bidding for it, and I'll bid all the way. When your Bowl comes up, set any minimum you like, and the table can watch you charge me for a set you'll never finish.",
   "Graham: If I let her have the Jar I can never finish, so I'm not taking that deal; she can't go past her 45, so 46 settles it. P15 is out of the soldiers' reach for two years, and Rob's man at S14 is one square from my brush, so I'm paying more attention to him than to her threats."),
 B(6, 22),
 B(7, 23),
 B(6, None, "Keep it, Graham. Just remember who's selling the Bowl next year.")],
 hover="Graham puts the Jar on P15.", at="P15")
say("Silke", "The Brush, at two. Paddy let me have one at two last year, so that's him paid back in kind. Graham, the Bowl comes up next year. Your Scroll comes up in a minute, so set it at two and the Bowl goes up at two, your word for my word. Set it high and you'll find out exactly how long a year is.",
 "Silke: I've just held back the one piece Graham needs to win, so now he has to sit and wait with Rob's soldier one square from his Brush. He'll go easy on that Scroll.")
act("Silke puts up the Bamboo Brush, minimum 2, and keeps the Wooden Bowl back for next year, when it must go up. Paddy needs a Brush; Graham has one. The Bowl is now the last piece Graham needs.", map_hl=[6,5,7])
say("Graham", "The brush is yours, Paddy, I'll stay out of it. Silke, I'll take that deal: my Scroll at two now, your Bowl at two next year, in front of Reina's book. I kept the last one we made, so check the list if you doubt me.",
 "Graham: I don't need a second brush, and a Scroll at two costs me nothing, while getting the Bowl at two finishes my set. The real danger now is Rob's man on S14, one step from my brush, and that's who I'll be watching all year.")
say("Rob", "Have it, Paddy, you're right, I can't paint. Look at the three of you, though, all square and shaking hands and writing everything in Reina's book. Graham, you're one bowl short now, and you've got your jar, your brush and your customs house all sitting in a row on P15, Q15 and R14. That's a lovely little shop window for my lad on S14.",
 "Rob: Graham's put three pieces right on W3's doorstep, so next march I take the brush and tick off Graham. W1 is two steps from Paddy's customs house, so that's two of my three names as good as crossed off.")
lot(6, "BB", [
 B(5, 2, "Thank you, Silke, that's a kind turn, and I'll take it at two if nobody minds. You and I are square now on brushes. Rob, if you want to bid on a brush you can't use, go ahead, but you'll only be paying the Harvest Pile for the pleasure.",
   "Paddy: I don't need a full Silk set to keep my win, but a brush at two is cheap insurance, and P25 puts it out of reach of Rob's lads for two rounds. I'll go a little higher if Rob tries to make mischief, but not much.")],
 hover="Nobody else bids. Paddy puts the Brush on P25.", at="P25")
act("Paddy, now the Silk Peasant, holds a Customs House, the Scroll, the Vase and the Brush. Like Graham, he needs only the Wooden Bowl, the last piece in Silke's stock.", map_hl=[5,7,6])
say("Graham", "Two, Silke, as we agreed, and Reina has it in her book. Paddy, I see you need the Bowl now as well. Our deal sets the minimum at two, but it can't stop anyone bidding, so when it comes up I'll be bidding against you in the open, the same as you did to me over the Vase.",
 "Graham: I've kept my side, so if Silke goes back on the Bowl, the whole table saw who broke it first. The minimum was never the real problem, though: Paddy has seven more grain than I do and needs the same Bowl, so the fight is with him now.")
act("Graham puts up the Land Deed Scroll that came back to him from Silke, minimum 2. Silke, now the Rice Peasant, needs one; Paddy has his. Over the Vase last year Graham was the seller, and Paddy bid against Silke.", map_hl=[7,6])
say("Paddy", "It's Silke's scroll, I've already got one. And Graham, so you can stop fretting, I'll stay out of that Bowl next year. A full set wouldn't do me any good, since the only seats I could swap into are further down the coast than the one I've got. It's between you and Silke, and you've already made your deal.",
 "Paddy: Any swap from here sends me to a higher seat, which is a loss, so the Bowl's worth nothing to me. I may as well promise it away in public and save everyone a bidding war. Better the table's grain goes into the works than into the Harvest Pile over a pot I'd never use.")
say("Rob", "Go on, Silke, take it for two. Get something back on the board, love, you've had nothing out there since Paddy moved in on your coast. Listen to the three of you, correcting each other's minutes. You'll be electing a chairman next.",
 "Rob: Silke's the only name on my list with nothing on the board, so once that scroll goes down in the rice fields I've got a target for W2. A cheap scroll for her is a free one for me.")
lot(7, "LD", [
 B(6, 2, "Two it is, Graham, and the Bowl goes up at two next year, I'm not taking that back. And thank you, Paddy, for standing out of it. Mind you, Graham, Reina's book has you down for the Vase, not Paddy. Paddy bid against me, not against you. Let's keep the record straight if we're all going to swear by it.",
   "Silke: That's my first piece in the rice fields, and it's going on Q25, the one corner Rob's soldiers can't reach for two years. Graham gets his Bowl at two, but now he owes me one, and I'll be collecting on that.")],
 hover="Nobody else bids. Silke puts the Scroll on Q25.", at="Q25")
march({"W1":"O21", "W2":"Q23", "W3":"R14"}, "Robert Land marches: W3 steps onto R14 and takes Graham's Bamboo Brush, the first piece he has raided; it goes onto his desert at T14. W1 steps onto O21 and raids Paddy's Province 5; W2 steps onto Q23 and raids Silke's Province 6. Five from each.",
 "Rob: That's the first piece in the bag, Graham's brush on R14, so one name's crossed off. W1 sits next to Paddy's customs house and W2 is two steps from Silke's new scroll, so next year I could sweep all three.",
 take={"W3": ("BB", "T14")})
say("Rob", "Graham, my lad's just come through your garden gate and he's very taken with your brush. He'll be keeping it. Paddy, he's one step from your customs house now, so put the kettle on. And Silke, lovely scroll, that. It'd look even better in the desert.")
salaries({7:5}, text="Reina pays Graham his 5 for reading the cards.")
act("A table ruling from Manish, taking effect now: with no Official, Gentry or Scholar at this table, the Silk Peasant stands in for the upper class. A Silk Peasant who completes the Silk set (a Scroll, a Brush, the Vase and the Bowl) and holds 20 grain may take the throne: they return their realia, the Emperor hands over the whole reserve, draws 60 and takes the Silk seat. The Rice and Wheat Peasants still climb to Silk first.", map_hl=[1,5])

# ===== CHAPTER 3: Next Year's Another Year
# ============================== YEAR 5 ==============================
R(5, "The villages grow again", "half1")
act("Graham turns the I Ching card: Population Grows by Half. The villages have already grown by half once, so only the good harvest counts: every province collects a good harvest and eats what it ate last year.", map_hl=[1,5,6,7,8])
harvest("Silke pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile.")
say("Reina", "Forty percent, five years running. Write it down, Silke. The canal goes down the coast as I promised, and it starts in the rice fields, because the rice fields have had the worst of it. And Paddy, my dear, you told this whole table you'd stay out of the Bowl, and that's in my book too. I'll only say that if anyone at this table bids on that Bowl to get a seat they never mentioned, they'll find the Emperor bidding against them, and I have the deepest pockets in China. Rob, darling, you're refusing again, I assume? Year five on the tally.",
 "Reina: Under Manish's new ruling, the Bowl is the last piece Paddy needs for my chair, and he swore off it before he knew that. So I'm holding him to it in front of everyone, and I'm putting the canal in Silke's province, not his, to keep her afloat and keep her annoyed with him.")
taxes(.40, collector="Paddy collects the tax for the crown")
act("Robert Land doesn't pay.", map_hl=[8])
build("Grand Canal", 40, {1:40}, canal={6:1}, text="Public works: Reina digs the first section of the Grand Canal in Province 6, Silke's rice fields. It adds 10 to Silke's harvest from next year.", hover="Reina pays the 40. 360 of the works are still owed.")
act("A reminder from the rules: the Emperor may not bid at auction. The Wooden Bowl, the only one, is the last piece Paddy needs for a Silk set, and with that set and 20 grain the Silk Peasant may take the throne.", map_hl=[1,5])
say("Silke", "Two. I said two when Graham gave me his Scroll, and I said I wasn't taking it back, so I'm not. Paddy, you told the whole table you'd stay out of this Bowl, and now the Bowl is a throne. And Reina, Section 10 says you can't bid, so all that's guarding your seat now is Paddy's word. Let's find out what it's worth.",
 "Silke: I keep my promises even when they cost me. Now everyone at this table gets to watch whether Paddy keeps his when breaking it would make him Emperor. If he bids, I take a quarter of whatever he pays, and the Emperor's whole book turns on him instead of me.")
act("Silke puts up the Wooden Bowl, the last piece in her stock and the only Bowl, at the minimum, 2, as she promised Graham last year in return for his Scroll at 2. Paddy needs it to finish his Silk set; last year he promised the table he'd stay out of it. Graham needs it too, and a Brush.", map_hl=[6,5,7])
say("Paddy", "I said I'd stay out of the Bowl, and I meant it when I said it, so I'm staying out of it. I know what it's worth now, Silke, you needn't tell me. But a man who breaks his word the minute it gets expensive was never worth the trust in the first place. Graham, it's yours at two if nobody else wants it, and good luck to you. Next year's another year, though, and I never promised anything about next year.",
 "Paddy: That's the hardest pass I've ever made. The throne's sitting right there for two grain. But the whole table trusts me because I kept my word on the brushes and the Vase, and Graham still needs a Brush before that Bowl does him any good. If it comes up again next year, my promise will have run out.")
say("Rob", "Pass, five years running, Reina, write it down next to the tax. I don't buy pots, Graham, I collect them. Two grain for the only bowl in China, and Paddy keeping his word in public? I'd like that in the committee's minutes, please, before somebody corrects them.",
 "Rob: That bowl is worth nothing to me in my hand. It's only worth something sitting out on a square where my lads can walk over and pick it up. W1 is one step from Paddy's customs house and W2 is two from Silke's scroll, so let the farmers settle their crockery while my boys finish the walk.")
lot(6, "WB", [
 B(7, 2, "Silke's right, and I'll take the correction: the Vase was Paddy against her, not Paddy against me. I'll fix my list tonight. Two is what we agreed, Silke, and thank you, Paddy. I'll remember that you said it and then did it. Rob, if your man fancies a bowl as well, he'll be paying a lot more than two for it.",
   "Graham: Paddy just went onto the good list, and Silke's been on it all year. I've put it on P17 where none of Rob's lads can reach it, and the only thing I'm short of now is a brush, which is sitting on Rob's desert.")],
 hover="Paddy and Rob pass. Graham puts the Bowl on P17.", at="P17")
act("Graham holds a Customs House, the Dragon Jar and the Wooden Bowl. He still needs a Brush: Paddy has one on P25, and the other is the one Rob's soldier took from Graham, now on Rob's desert at T14.", map_hl=[7,5,8])
march({"W1":"O22", "W2":"Q24", "W3":"R15"}, "Robert Land marches: W1 steps onto O22 and takes Paddy's Customs House, the second piece he has raided; it goes onto his desert at O18. W2 steps onto Q24 and raids Silke's Province 6; W3 steps onto R15 and raids Graham's Province 7. Five from each.",
 "Rob: W1 lifts Paddy's customs house, so that's two of the three names crossed off. W2 is one step from Silke's scroll, and W3 hangs about by my desert so nobody gets ideas about the brush Graham still needs.",
 take={"W1": ("CH", "O18")})
say("Rob", "Paddy, your customs house is under new management, and my man's very good with paperwork. Silke, that's five for the visitor's fee, and the scroll's next on his list. Graham, congratulations on the bowl. My lad's just come to admire it and leave a small bill.")
act("The Silk set needs no Customs House, so Paddy still holds the Scroll, the Vase and a Brush, and still needs only the Wooden Bowl, which Graham holds on P17. Rob now holds two raided pieces, one each from Graham and Paddy; Silke's Scroll on Q25 is one step from W2.", map_hl=[5,7,8])
salaries({7:5}, text="Reina pays Graham his 5 for reading the cards.")

# ============================== YEAR 6 ==============================
R(6, "Fat years make large families", "double1")
act("Graham turns the I Ching card: Population Doubles. Every province collects a good harvest, and from this year every province eats twice what it ate. With the population doubled, new harvest squares may now be bought.", map_hl=[1,5,6,7,8])
harvest("Silke pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile.")
say("Reina", "Forty percent, six years running, and yes, Silke, it raises nothing, and yes, it's in the book anyway. The crown ate sixty this year, the same as all of you went hungry, so no works and no spending until the granary fills again. Graham, your five is suspended for one lean year, not cancelled, and I'll pay it back when the harvest turns. Anyone who buys new land gets five a square from me, and that's the whole of my charity. Rob, darling, year six on the tally.",
 "Reina: Sixty grain and the population doubled, so this is the year the Emperor stops looking generous and starts looking solvent. Paddy needs a Customs House now, and the nearest one is Graham's on Q15 with Rob's lad standing right next to it, so the pots are still keeping my seat warm for me, and I don't need to spend a grain.")
taxes(.40, collector="Paddy collects the tax for the crown")
act("Every province ate more than it grew, so the tax raises nothing. Robert Land doesn't pay in any case. No public work this year: 360 of the works are still owed.", map_hl=[1,8])
act("There are no auctions this year: every piece has been sold. Before Rob marches, each peasant in turn may buy new land (20 a square; Reina has offered 5 a square toward it), buy a soldier from Rob, or offer a deal.", map_hl=[5,6,7])
say("Paddy", "My promise was for Year 5, and I kept it to the letter. You all saw me do it. So here it is in the open, Graham: forty grain for your Bowl, today. That's twenty times what you paid. You need a Brush to finish, and there are only two in China, mine and the one in Rob's desert, so that Bowl's worth more to me than it'll ever be to you. Reina, I'm sorry, but I did say next year was another year.",
 "Paddy: If Graham takes it, I've got the Bowl and twenty-six and a half grain, and that's enough for the throne. If he won't, I'll keep my Brush close, because that Brush is the only thing standing between Graham and my seat.")
act("Paddy offers Graham 40 grain for the Wooden Bowl, paid at once. With the Bowl, Paddy would hold a complete Silk set (the Silk set needs no Customs House), and with 20 grain he could take the throne.", map_hl=[5,7,1])
say("Silke", "Rob, I'll have one of your lads, and he'll be standing on my scroll, so yours can come and knock if he likes. And Graham, forty's a lovely number, but you're not selling Paddy a bowl, you're selling him the throne. Ask yourself what the Emperor charges the man who sold it to him.",
 "Silke: W2 is one step from the only piece I've got, so twenty grain to keep it is cheaper than starting from nothing a third time. Paddy kept his word to the letter and then found the loophole straight away. I'd rather Graham felt that on his own skin before he takes the forty.")
sell_soldiers([(6,"Q25")], "Silke buys a soldier from Rob and stands it on Q25, on her Land Deed Scroll, to guard it. Rob's W2 stands next to it on Q24.", "Rob keeps 5 of the 20; 15 goes to the Harvest Pile.")
say("Graham", "No, Paddy. You kept your word for exactly one year and I've written down that you did, but the Bowl isn't for sale at forty or at any price. Rob, you said you collect pots and don't buy them. Then sell me one back: thirty grain, today, for the brush your lad took off my garden. That's more than anyone at this table has ever paid you for a raid.",
 "Graham: Paddy needs my Bowl and I need a brush, and Rob's holding the only spare one in China, so the throne goes to whichever of us he deals with. Thirty leaves me thirty-eight, which still covers the twenty I'd need to climb, so the sum works out.")
act("Graham refuses Paddy's offer and offers Rob 30 grain for the Bamboo Brush on Rob's desert at T14, the one Rob's soldier took from Graham's R14 two years ago. It is the last piece Graham needs for a Wheat set.", map_hl=[7,8,5])
act("Rob accepts Graham's offer. Graham pays Rob 30 grain, and the Bamboo Brush goes from Rob's desert at T14 to Graham's P18.", "A private trade: nothing goes to the Harvest Pile.", {7: -30, 8: 30}, map_hl=[7,8], deal=True)
give(pid(8), pid(7), "BB", "P18")
act("Graham now holds a Customs House, the Bamboo Brush, the Dragon Jar and the Wooden Bowl: a complete Wheat set, with 38 grain.", map_hl=[7])
march({"W1":"O23", "W2":"Q25", "W3":"Q16"}, "Robert Land marches: W2 steps onto Q25, captures Silke's soldier straight on and takes Silke's Land Deed Scroll, his third raided piece, one from each peasant. W1 steps onto O23 and takes Paddy's Land Deed Scroll. W3 steps onto Q16 and raids Graham's Province 7 for 5. Both Scrolls go onto Rob's desert, Silke's at T24 and Paddy's at N18.",
 "Rob: W2 takes Silke's scroll and her guard with it, which completes my list: Graham, Paddy and Silke, all three crossed off, and Graham's paid me thirty for the privilege. W1 lifts Paddy's scroll so his silk set falls apart again, and W3 strolls toward that famous Bowl, just to watch everyone sweat.",
 take={"W2": ("LD", "T24"), "W1": ("LD", "N18")})
say("Rob", "Thirty, Graham? Done. I did say it was a lovely thing to own and a lovely thing to lose, and I never said who'd be losing it. Silke, your lad's a credit to you, he stood his ground beautifully and now he works for me. And Paddy, your scroll's coming for a little walk in the desert, so do please correct the committee's minutes.")
say("Graham", "I'm taking the silk coast, Paddy. Silke kept every promise she made me, so I won't be the one who sends her inland. You told us next year was another year, and this is it. The Jar goes up for auction next year, and anyone who wants it can bid in the open. That's how I've played it all game.",
 "Graham: Ninety-nine and a half grain, on the seat that's only a Scroll and a Vase short of the throne, and I got there by moving off the list of people who kept their word onto the list of people who didn't. I'll be the quiet one at the table watching Rob, because he now owns every Scroll in China.")
swap(7, 5, "Graham Garner completes the Wheat set and replaces Paddy Doyle as the Silk Peasant. Graham takes Province 5 and Paddy's 66.5 grain, 99.5 in all; the Dragon Jar, Province 5's own piece, goes back into the Silk stock for Graham to auction, and his Customs House, Brush and Bowl stand on P19, P20 and N20. Paddy returns his Vase and Brush, draws 60 from the Harvest Pile and takes the Wheat seat, Province 7.",
 at={"CH": "P19", "BB": "P20", "WB": "N20"})
say("Rob", "Reina, darling, six years you kept a tally of my sins, so you'll know exactly where you put the book. Pass it here, and the keys to the Winter Palace while you're at it. Walls have long memories, you said. Let's see how long they are from the other side. The desert's yours now, and the lads are good boys, they only need feeding.",
 "Rob: Six years without paying a grain of tax, and now I'm the one who collects it, which is the best joke in China. The pots go back up for auction, the farmers bid each other into the ground, and three quarters of every price lands in my Harvest Pile.")
swap(8, 1, "Robert Land, the Warlord, has raided a piece from every peasant, and takes the throne. He returns his pieces (the Customs House to the Silk stock, both Scrolls to the Wheat stock, for auction) and takes Reina's whole reserve, 60, for 185 in all. Reina Osorio draws 60 from the Harvest Pile and becomes the Warlord, with W1, W2 and W3 where they stand.")
act("The table jobs stay where they were: Silke is banker, Graham card reader and Paddy tax collector. Reina had already suspended the card reader's 5 for this year, so no salaries are paid.", map_hl=[1,8])
act("The end of Year 6. The seats now: Rob Emperor (1), Graham Silk Peasant (5), Silke Rice Peasant (6), Paddy Wheat Peasant (7), Reina Warlord (8). Beside the board for next year's auctions: the Dragon Jar and a Customs House in Graham's stock, a Brush in Silke's, the Vase and both Scrolls in Paddy's.", map_hl=[1,5,6,7,8])
say("Rob", "Well, that's the year. Committee, the minutes will now show that the desert has a throne and the throne has a book. Reina, my love, the lads like their rations on time, and they don't care for long speeches about walls. Same tax rate as before, I'm told, so nobody can say I'm not a traditionalist.",
 "Rob: Fair cop, the auction money goes to the Harvest Pile and not my pocket, so the crown lives on tax and its own harvest, and I've just spent six years teaching everyone not to pay it. Three hundred and sixty of works still owed, a lean granary and Reina leading my old boys: it turns out the villain's seat was the comfy one.")
say("Graham", "Emperor Rob. I've had three different seats in six years, and each one cost less than the last. Paddy, the Vase and both Scrolls are in your stock now, so we'll have something to talk about next year. I'll keep it in the open, the way you did with the Bowl.",
 "Graham: I'm one Scroll and one Vase from the throne, and Paddy's holding all three of them, so next year's auction is between him and me. The man in the Winter Palace is the man who sold me back my own brush, and that one went on the first list.")
say("Silke", "Twenty-eight grain, no pieces, and the man who took my scroll now owns the book that recorded it. Fine. Graham, thank you for keeping me on the coast; I'll remember it. Rob, Your Majesty, I'm still the banker, so every grain you hand out goes through my hands first.",
 "Silke: Every piece is back up for auction now, and for once nobody at this table holds a full set. I've got the bank, the only Brush in the Rice stock, and a reputation for keeping my word, so next year everyone has to come to me.")
say("Paddy", "Well. I came here a rice farmer and I'm going home a wheat farmer with sixty grain and an empty field, so I suppose that's a step up. Fair play to you, Graham. You did it in the open and I can't fault you for that. Rob, God help us all. Reina, if those lads of yours need feeding, I've a Vase and two Scrolls to sell next year, and I'm not proud.",
 "Paddy: I had the throne two grain away and gave it up to keep my word, and the next year I lost the lot. Still, I've nothing left for a soldier to steal, three pieces to sell, and Graham owes me a kindness, so a farmer plants again.")
say("Reina", "Take the book, darling, it's all in there, and you'll find your tally runs out at year six. Mine starts at year seven. Enjoy the Winter Palace, Rob. Three hundred and sixty in works still owed, a population that's just doubled, and an empty Province 1 under a wall nobody's finished. I built you a lovely house. Now you get to pay for the rest of the street.",
 "Reina: I watched the Silk seat all year and walked straight into the Warlord's clause, and that one's on me, not on Rob. But I've got three lads, sixty grain, and a whole coast I know better than anyone here, so the new Emperor is about to find out what it's like to have the woman who drew up the wall plans standing outside it.")

# ===== CHAPTER 4: A Lovely Thing to Owe
# ============================== YEAR 7 ==============================
R(7, "The seed rots", "dribble3")
act("A table ruling from Manish, taking effect now: a new Warlord keeps the army where it stands and starts the objective afresh. Reina commands W1, W2 and W3; only raids she makes herself count. Her targets are every other player in a realia-holding seat (Graham, Silke and Paddy), and the Emperor is still left out, so Rob is safe while he holds the throne.", map_hl=[8,5,6,7])
act("Graham turns the I Ching card: Bad Harvest. Every province collects a bad harvest, and with the population doubled, every province eats more than it grows.", map_hl=[1,5,6,7,8])
harvest("Silke pays out the harvest; this year every province pays into the Harvest Pile instead.")
act("Every province pays the pile this year: Rob 110, Graham 70, Paddy 70, Silke 60 (her canal section earns her 10) and Reina 40. Silke falls to 32 in debt and Paddy to 10 in debt; neither holds a piece on the board to sell back. Until a debt is paid that player can't bid, buy, trade, give grain or change seats, and every grain they receive pays it first. A player in debt is a cause for revolt.", map_hl=[6,7])
say("Rob", "Right, committee, take this down. Tax stays the same, because I'm a traditionalist and because there's nothing to tax. The Emperor will now pay for something with his own grain, which I appreciate is a shock to everyone at this table, me included. Silke, that's your canal finished, forty grain, and it all goes through your hands, as you so kindly reminded me. Graham, you're still reading the cards and I'm paying you five for it, because I'd like at least one of you to be able to afford a cup of tea. Paddy, the Reservoir's there next year if you can stay out of trouble. Lovely thing to own, a canal. Very hard to start a revolt about.",
 "Rob: Two in debt and two votes needed for a revolt, so I'm buying the loudest debtor a canal and the only solvent farmer a wage, and leaving Paddy to wait for his turn. Turns out the villain's real job is keeping everyone just happy enough not to agree on anything.")
taxes(.40, collector="Paddy collects the tax for the crown")
act("Every income is negative, so the tax raises nothing.", map_hl=[1])
build("Grand Canal", 40, {1:40}, canal={6:1}, text="Public works: Rob digs the second and last Grand Canal section in Province 6, Silke's rice fields. It adds another 10 to her harvest from next year.", hover="Rob pays the 40 from his own grain. 320 of the works are still owed.")
act("Rob keeps the table jobs as they are (Silke banker, Graham card reader, Paddy tax collector) and will pay Graham his 5 for reading the cards. He offers the Province 7 Reservoir next year, to Paddy's province, if Paddy \"stays out of trouble\"; no land is bought.", map_hl=[1,5,6,7])
say("Graham", "The Customs House, at two. Reina, you're the only one at this table who can lift a hand, so it's yours at two if you want a building in the sand. I'm keeping the Jar. Paddy, once you're out of the red, the Jar's on the table for a Scroll, straight swap, and I'll say that again next year in front of everyone.",
 "Graham: I've already got a Customs House on P19, so the spare is worth half a grain to me and nothing at all to the two people who are broke. The Jar is the one thing both of them still need, and a man with a list holds on to his leverage.")
act("Graham puts up his spare Customs House, minimum 2, and keeps the Dragon Jar back for next year, when it must go up. Silke and Paddy both need a Customs House, but both are in debt and can't bid, and the Emperor doesn't bid, so only Reina can.", map_hl=[5,8])
lot(5, "CH", [
 B(8, 2, "Two grain for a house in the sand, Graham? Sold, darling. I've built palaces for less gratitude. Rob, write it in the book: the desert now has a building and a throne, and only one of those is mine.",
   "Reina: Nobody else at this table can lift a hand, so this costs me two and gives me a piece I can sell back the next time the harvest bites. It also keeps Graham friendly while my lads work out which of his three pieces is closest to W1.")],
 hover="Nobody else may bid. Reina puts the Customs House on her desert at O18. A piece the Warlord buys counts nothing toward her objective.", at="O18")
say("Silke", "The only Brush in the Rice stock, minimum fifteen. Reina, it's no use to you in the sand, and Graham, you've got one already, so if either of you pays fifteen just to keep it away from Paddy, everyone at this table will know exactly why you did it. Otherwise it stays with me until Paddy's out of the red, and then he and I will talk.",
 "Silke: Neither of the two who can bid actually needs it, so fifteen means it either comes back to me for Paddy next year or somebody pays my debt down to keep it from him. I'm happy either way, and I know which of them I'm watching.")
act("Silke puts up the Bamboo Brush, the last piece in her stock, minimum 15. Paddy needs a Brush but is in debt; Graham already has one. Only Graham and Reina may bid. If nobody does, the Brush stays in her stock and must go up again next year.", map_hl=[6,7])
say("Graham", "I've got a brush already, Silke, and I paid for it twice. I'm not paying fifteen for a third one just to make a point. Paddy can have it when he's back on his feet.",
 "Graham: Fifteen out of thirty would leave me under the twenty I need to take the throne, with the Vase and a Scroll still to buy. Blocking Paddy isn't worth losing the whole game over, and I'd rather he owed me for standing aside.")
say("Reina", "Fifteen for a brush I'd only use to sweep sand? No, Silke, keep it for Paddy. I'd much rather see it planted in his wheat field where everyone can admire it.",
 "Reina: Every piece that ends up on a peasant's board is something for my lads to walk towards, so I'm happy for Paddy to own that Brush. I'm keeping my eighteen grain for rations.")
act("Nobody bids. The Bamboo Brush stays in Silke's stock and goes up again next year.", map_hl=[6])
say("Paddy", "One Scroll, lads, minimum ten. The Vase stays with me until Silke can bid on it as well. It wouldn't be fair to sell the only one while half the table has its hands tied, and I'm not going to start doing things unfairly now I'm the one in the red. Graham, I heard you about the Jar for my other Scroll. I'll hold you to it in front of everyone the day I'm square.",
 "Paddy: Ten grain only knocks two and a half off my debt, but it gets a spare Scroll moving and I keep the other one for Graham's Jar. And the Vase is worth more with two buyers fighting over it, so I'll wait till Silke can bid.")
act("Paddy puts up one of his two Land Deed Scrolls, minimum 10, and keeps the Vase and the other Scroll in his stock. Graham needs a Scroll and the Vase to finish the Silk set; Silke needs both too but is in debt. Only Graham and Reina may bid.", map_hl=[7,5,6])
say("Reina", "A land deed in the desert, Paddy? I hold the whole desert already and nobody's ever asked to see the paperwork. Graham, it's yours if you want it. Just don't stand it too far from my lads, they get lonely.",
 "Reina: Whatever Graham buys goes on the coast, and that's the direction my soldiers are walking anyway. I'd rather let him pay to lay the table for me.")
lot(7, "LD", [
 B(5, 10, "Ten for the Scroll, Paddy, at your price, and thank you for not making the Vase a race nobody else can run. Reina, a land deed's no use in the desert. There's no land out there to deed.",
   "Graham: Ten leaves me exactly the twenty the throne asks for, and I'll only go past that if Reina's bidding to block me, not to own it. It goes on L24, where none of her soldiers can reach it for two rounds.")],
 hover="Reina passes. Graham puts the Scroll on L24. Paddy's 2.5 pays down his debt.", at="L24")
act("Graham holds a Customs House, the Bamboo Brush, the Wooden Bowl and now a Land Deed Scroll, with 20 grain. For the Silk set, and the throne, he needs only the Vase, which Paddy keeps in his stock until next year.", map_hl=[5,7,1])
march({"W1":"N22", "W2":"Q24", "W3":"P17"}, "Reina Osorio marches: W3 steps onto P17 and raids Paddy's Province 7 for 5, deepening his debt. W1 steps onto N22, in empty Province 4, which raids nothing; W2 steps back onto Q24, a square already raided.",
 "Reina: W1 cuts through empty Province 4 so it's three steps from the Bowl, the Brush and the Scroll all at once, and W3 picks up rations on its way to the Customs House. Silke and Paddy hold nothing yet, so for now Graham's the only one who can give me a piece.")
say("Reina", "Paddy, darling, you offered to feed my lads, and they've taken you at your word. Five from the wheat fields, and I'll remember the kindness. Graham, don't look so worried. Nobody's walking to the coast this year. Probably.")
salaries({5:5}, text="Rob pays Graham his 5 for reading the cards.")
act("The end of Year 7. Silke is 32 in debt and Paddy 12.5. Graham holds the Customs House, Brush, Bowl and a Scroll, with 25 grain; the Vase in Paddy's stock is the last piece of his Silk set. Still beside the board: the Dragon Jar in Graham's stock, the Brush in Silke's, the Vase and a Scroll in Paddy's.", map_hl=[5,6,7])

# ============================== YEAR 8 ==============================
R(8, "The Emperor's share", "share")
act("Graham turns the I Ching card: The Emperor's Share. Every province collects an average harvest, and every province with a public work splits the extra harvest it produces 50/50 with the Emperor this year. Silke's two canal sections add 20 to her harvest, so she owes Rob 10.", map_hl=[1,5,6,7,8])
act("The harvest is average, and every province eats more than it grows: Rob 85, Graham 45, Paddy 45, Reina 30 and Silke 25 (her canal earns her 20). Graham has 25 and owes 45, so before he pays he must sell realia back to the Harvest Pile at 2 each until the payment is covered or he has none left: all four of his pieces cover only 8. Reina has 23 and owes 30, and sells back her one piece.", map_hl=[5,8])
sell_back(pid(5), ["CH","BB","WB","LD"], "Graham sells his Customs House, Bamboo Brush, Wooden Bowl and Land Deed Scroll back to the Harvest Pile at 2 each, 8 in all. They lift off P19, P20, N20 and L24 into the pile's own stock, which it auctions after the provinces' lots.", "Sell before you sink: a payment that would take a player below zero is first covered by selling realia back at 2 a piece.")
sell_back(pid(8), ["CH"], "Reina sells the Customs House on O18 back to the Harvest Pile for 2.")
harvest("Silke pays out the harvest; this year every province pays into the Harvest Pile instead.")
act("The Emperor's Share: Silke pays Rob half of her canal's 20, which is 10. It deepens her debt and pays down his.", None, {pid(6): -10, pid(1): 10}, map_hl=[6,1])
act("Every player is now in debt to the pile: Rob 45, Graham 12, Reina 5, Paddy 57.5 and Silke 67. In debt nobody may bid, buy, trade, give grain, pay toward a public work or change seats, and every grain received pays the debt first. Debt is a cause for revolt.", map_hl=[1,5,6,7,8])
say("Rob", "Well, committee, the minutes will show that for the first time in eight years this table is in perfect agreement: every one of us is broke. Debt is a cause for revolt, I'm told, and I'd love to see the two of you who are clean enough to cast the first stone, so please step forward. Nobody? Then the throne stays where it is, the jobs stay where they are, and Graham, your five goes in the book as owed and not forgotten. Lovely thing to own, an empire. Lovely thing to owe.",
 "Rob: When everyone's in the red, nobody can point a finger without three pointing back at them, so I'm sitting very still on this throne and hoping the next card is kinder than the last two. If the table talks itself into collapse, at least I'll go down as the only villain who ever paid for a canal.")
taxes(.40, collector="Paddy collects the tax for the crown")
act("Every income is negative, so the tax raises nothing. The crown is in debt and can't pay toward a public work, so none is built: 320 of the works are still owed. Rob keeps the table jobs; Graham's 5 goes unpaid while the crown is in debt.", map_hl=[1,5])
act("A note from the rules: being in debt doesn't stop a player revolting or agreeing to a revolt. The player in debt may be the one who revolts, and two other players must agree a cause exists.", map_hl=[1,5,6,7,8])
say("Graham", "The Jar, at two. Nobody can bid, so it'll come back to me, and that's fine, because it's the only thing I've got left. Rob, I heard the rule just as you did. A cause exists. I'm not calling it this year, and I'm saying so in front of everyone so it goes in the book as well.",
 "Graham: Four pieces sold back for eight grain. That's six years' work, and the only one who paid for it was the weather. Rob is on the good list, but he's forty-five in debt on a throne I was one Vase short of, and I've written that down too.")
act("Graham puts up the Dragon Jar, the last piece in his stock, minimum 2. Every player is in debt, so nobody may bid, and the Jar stays in his stock.", map_hl=[5])
say("Silke", "The Brush at two. Nobody can bid, so it comes back to me, and that's fine. Now look at the bank, because I'm the one holding it. Every seat at this table owes the pile, the crown can't lay a stone, and the works are still three hundred and twenty short. I'm sixty-seven down and I'm not too proud to say it: this China is finished. I propose we give up under Section 18, collapse and no winners, so nobody walks off with a throne they got by being the last one to drown. Graham, Paddy, Rob, Reina, that's my proposal. If you vote no, tell me which of you thinks this sum ever comes right.",
 "Silke: I'm deepest in the red, so a game with no winners is the best result left for me, and I'd much rather see it end level than watch Graham buy a Vase on the day he's back in the black. If they turn it down, they've told me who still thinks they can win, and that's the person I bank against.")
act("Silke puts up the Bamboo Brush, minimum 2; nobody may bid, and it stays in her stock. Then she proposes that the table give up: under Section 18 the game would end in collapse, with no winners. The table stops only if every player agrees.", map_hl=[6,1,5,7,8])
say("Paddy", "Yes, Silke. I've done the sum three times on the back of my hand and it never comes out. I'm fifty-seven and a half down, and my fields eat forty-five more than they grow every year. Nobody wins a race when we're all at the bottom of the river. If anyone votes no, the Vase is up at two, and it'll be up at two in the open every year until somebody can pay for it. That's a promise, and you all know what my promises are worth, for better or worse.",
 "Paddy: My father said a good name is worth more than a full barn, and it's just as well, because the barn's gone. I won't hang on to a game that's already finished just because I'm holding the one piece Graham needs.")
act("Paddy agrees to give up. If the game goes on, he puts up the Vase at 2, which nobody may bid on.", map_hl=[7])
say("Reina", "Silke, I've been doing that sum since Year 1, and I've never once had to lie to myself about it. My desert eats thirty more than it grows, my lads can't steal a piece nobody owns, and the crown can't lay a single stone. Yes. Rob, darling, the minutes can show that the last thing this table agreed on was the truth.",
 "Reina: I'm only five down, the richest woman in a bankrupt China, and I could keep raiding the drowning for a few more years, but there's no year on the calendar where it comes right. A manager knows when the plan is finished, and I'd rather sign the collapse than be the last one standing on the wreck.")
act("Reina agrees to give up. If the game goes on, her three soldiers stand where they are.", map_hl=[8])
say("Rob", "Fair cop on the rule, Storyteller, I was hoping nobody would read Section 16 that closely. Silke, I've done your sum too, and the throne eats eighty-five more than it grows, which is more than any of you, so the villain votes yes. Let the minutes show that the committee finally agreed on something, and it was that I'd won a country with nothing left in it. Lovely thing to own, an empire. Lovely thing to lose. I just never thought we'd all be losing it together.",
 "Rob: I spent six years teaching China not to pay for its walls and canals, and now there's nobody left to pay for them. I'd rather go down in the book as the villain who voted for the truth than as the last man sitting on a throne at the bottom of the river.")
act("Rob agrees to give up. Four of the five have said yes; Graham answers last.", map_hl=[1,5])
say("Graham", "Yes. I was one Vase short of the throne with exactly twenty in hand, and the weather took all of it in one card, so I won't pretend there's a sum here that comes right. For the minutes, Rob: everyone at this table kept their word to me this year. I didn't expect to write that twice.",
 "Graham: Eight years, three seats, and the only list that came out even was the one about who kept their word. I'll take that home instead of a throne.")
act("Every player agrees: the table gives up in Year 8. The game ends in collapse (Section 18), and a collapse has no winners. 320 of the 520 grain of public works were still owed.", map_hl=[1,5,6,7,8], big=True)
act("Where the game ends: Rob Emperor (drew the Warlord), Graham Silk Peasant (drew Wheat), Silke Rice Peasant (drew Silk), Paddy Wheat Peasant (drew Rice), Reina Warlord (drew the Emperor). Every player ends in debt to the Harvest Pile: Reina 5, Graham 12, Rob 45, Paddy 57.5, Silke 67. Nothing stands on the board.", map_hl=[1,5,6,7,8])
say("Rob", "Well, that's China done, and I'd like the minutes to show that the last Emperor's only public work was Silke's canal, and it was the only thing on this board still paying anyone at the end. Graham, your five is still in the book, and Reina, you were right: you built me a lovely house, and I couldn't afford the street. Same time next week? I'll bring the biscuits and none of you will have to pay tax on them.",
 "Rob: Six years as the villain, two as the Emperor, and I'm going home forty-five grain in debt with a throne nobody can sit on. Best night I've ever had at a board game, and that's how you know I was playing the right seat.")
say("Graham", "Twelve down, and the second-least in debt at a table where nobody won. I've had four seats in eight years, and each one cost less than the last, until the last one cost everything. Thank you all. I'm keeping the Jar.",
 "Graham: In the end the list was the only thing I held on to that the weather couldn't take. Paddy kept his word, Silke kept hers, and so did Rob, which nobody would have bet on.")
act("For the record: Graham held two seats, the Wheat Peasant's from the start and the Silk Peasant's from Year 6.", map_hl=[5,7])
say("Silke", "Then the bank closes its books. Sixty-seven down, one Brush nobody ever needed at the right moment, and every one of you kept your word to me at the very end. Rob, write it in the minutes: the Rice Peasant called time, and nobody at this table argued with the sum.",
 "Silke: I started with the bank and the only Bowl in China, and I'm finishing as the deepest in debt with a Brush in my hand. Still, nobody won on my grain, and the last thing this table did was what I asked it to.")
say("Paddy", "Well, there it is. Five of us went down together, and every one of us told the truth on the way. Graham, the Vase was always going to be yours at a fair price. I'm sorry the weather got to the auction before you did. Thanks for a grand game, all of you. I'll buy the tea, as soon as I'm out of the red.",
 "Paddy: I started on the rice coast, got the Silk seat once and lost it, and I'm finishing as a wheat farmer fifty-seven and a half in debt. I kept my word the whole way through, though, so I suppose we'll never know whether my father was right about the barn.")
say("Reina", "Two palaces, three canals and a desert full of soldiers with nothing left to steal. Rob, darling, I built you a lovely house, and in the end none of us could pay for the street. Thank you, all of you. It was the best-run collapse I've ever had the honour to sit through.",
 "Reina: I finished five down, the least broke at a table where everyone went under, and that's as close to winning as this China was ever going to let anyone get. The walls were sound, though. It was the weather that never added up.")
act("The table stops here, in Year 8, in economic collapse. Every player is in debt to the Harvest Pile, so no public work can be paid for, and 320 of the works can never be built.", map_hl=[1,5,6,7,8], big=True)
RESULT = export("The game ended in economic collapse in Year 8, so there are no winners (Section 18). The verdicts below compare each player's seat at the end with the seat they drew, for the record only.",
 "Eight years, five players, four changes of seat. The population grew by half in Year 3 and doubled in Year 6, and from Year 7 every province ate more than it could grow. Two palaces and four canal sections were built; the Great Wall, the Pagoda, the Reservoir and the Province 5 canal never were.")
